-- Migration: Question generation — AUD batch 69 (30 questions)
-- Date: 2026-02-28
-- Topic: Written Representations
-- Difficulty: 9E / 15M / 6H
-- Bloom's: L1:8 / L2:9 / L3:8 / L4:5

BEGIN;

INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L3 — Effect of completeness representation on planned substantive procedures
(1, 'Written Representations', 'During the audit of Silverton Financial Services for the year ended December 31, 2025, the engagement team at Caldwell & Associates obtains management''s written representation that all transactions have been recorded and all information has been provided. The senior auditor asks how this completeness representation affects planned substantive procedures. What is the most likely effect of obtaining the completeness representation on the auditor''s planned procedures?', '["The auditor may reduce the extent of substantive procedures for completeness assertions", "The auditor must still perform substantive procedures because written representations alone are insufficient evidence", "The auditor may eliminate testing of completeness-related assertions entirely", "The auditor should increase substantive testing to corroborate the completeness claim"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 580.04, written representations provide necessary audit evidence but do not substitute for other audit procedures the auditor considers necessary. Even after obtaining the completeness representation, the auditor must still perform substantive procedures because representations alone are insufficient for any assertion. Choice A is incorrect because obtaining a representation does not justify reducing planned substantive procedures — representations complement rather than replace other evidence.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Contradictions between representations and other audit evidence
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Northgate Capital Group provides its management representation letter to the engagement team at Drake & Partners for the December 31, 2025 audit. The letter states that there are no material contingent liabilities. However, the auditor''s review of board minutes and legal correspondence reveals a probable $1.2 million product liability claim. How should the auditor analyze this inconsistency between the written representation and other audit evidence?', '["Accept the written representation because management has ultimate knowledge of contingencies", "Consider the inconsistency as evidence that the written representations may not be reliable and investigate further", "Immediately issue an adverse opinion based on the conflicting evidence", "Disregard the written representation and rely solely on the documentary evidence"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 580.A27, when written representations are inconsistent with other audit evidence, the auditor should perform additional procedures to resolve the inconsistency and reconsider the reliability of management''s representations overall. The auditor must investigate the discrepancy rather than automatically accepting either source. Choice A is incorrect because representations do not override contradictory documentary evidence — the auditor must exercise professional skepticism and evaluate all available evidence.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Partial refusal to provide one specific required representation
(1, 'Written Representations', 'The audit of Hargrove Industries for the year ended December 31, 2025 is nearly complete. Management provides the representation letter to the engagement team at Monroe & Whitfield but refuses to include the representation about completeness of information provided to the auditor. Management states the refusal is due to concerns about potential regulatory scrutiny. All other representations are included and management has been cooperative throughout the engagement. The engagement partner must evaluate the appropriate response. What is the most appropriate course of action?', '["Disclaim the opinion because the completeness representation is a required representation under AU-C 580", "Accept the letter without the completeness representation and note the omission in the audit documentation", "Issue a qualified opinion because the limitation affects only one specific representation", "Issue an unmodified opinion with an emphasis-of-matter paragraph describing the limitation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 580.20, if management does not provide one or more of the requested written representations, the auditor should disclaim an opinion on the financial statements. The completeness of information representation is required, and its absence constitutes a scope limitation so severe that a disclaimer is mandatory regardless of management''s cooperation in other areas. Choice C is incorrect because a partial refusal to provide required representations cannot be addressed through qualification — the omission of a required representation necessitates a disclaimer.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — Management competence and reliability of specific representations
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Baxter Medical Devices has a newly hired controller with limited experience in medical device revenue recognition. During the audit for the year ended December 31, 2025, the engagement team at Franklin & Cole receives the management representation letter signed by the CEO and the new controller. The letter includes a representation that revenue is recognized in accordance with ASC 606. The senior auditor questions how the controller''s limited expertise affects this specific representation. What is the most likely impact?', '["The representation is invalid because the controller lacks sufficient expertise", "The auditor should obtain the representation from an independent third party instead", "The representation about revenue recognition is automatically excluded from the letter", "The auditor should consider the controller''s competence when assessing reliability and may need additional corroborating evidence"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 580.A8, the auditor considers whether the persons providing representations are competent and knowledgeable regarding the matters addressed. When management''s expertise on a specific topic is limited, the auditor may need to obtain additional corroborating evidence rather than relying heavily on that particular representation. Choice A is incorrect because limited expertise does not invalidate the representation — it remains a required element but the auditor adjusts the weight given to it.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Addressee of the management representation letter
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Under AU-C 580, the management representation letter should be addressed to:', '["The entity''s board of directors or audit committee", "The auditor or the audit firm conducting the engagement", "The Securities and Exchange Commission", "The entity''s shareholders or owners"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 580.14, the management representation letter is addressed to the auditor or the audit firm performing the engagement. The letter is a direct communication from management to the auditor confirming specific matters discussed during the audit. Choice A is incorrect because while the audit committee oversees the audit, the representation letter is a management-to-auditor communication, not a management-to-board communication.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Period covered by the representation letter for comparative statements
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Ridgeway Holdings presents comparative financial statements for the years ended December 31, 2025 and December 31, 2024. The engagement team at Sutton & Brooks is auditing both years. The controller asks what periods the management representation letter should cover. Under AU-C 580, the representation letter should cover:', '["Only the current year ended December 31, 2025", "Only the period from the prior year audit report date through the current report date", "All fiscal years since the entity''s incorporation", "Each period for which an audit opinion is being expressed on the financial statements"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 580.15, the management representation letter should cover all periods referred to in the auditor''s report. When comparative financial statements are presented and the auditor is reporting on both years, the representations must cover each period for which an opinion is expressed. Choice A is incorrect because limiting representations to only the current year would leave the prior-year financial statements without the required management assertions for that period.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Oral representations must be confirmed in writing
(1, 'Written Representations', 'During fieldwork for the December 31, 2025 audit of Westbrook Analytics, the CFO verbally assures the engagement team at Prescott & Lang that all related-party transactions have been disclosed. The audit manager asks whether this oral assurance is sufficient. Under AU-C 580, the auditor should:', '["Accept the oral assurance as sufficient evidence for related-party completeness", "Request that management include the related-party representation in the written representation letter", "Document the oral representation in a memorandum as an alternative to a written letter", "Require the CFO to sign a separate affidavit for each oral representation made"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 580.10, written representations are required for all representations that the auditor determines are necessary. Oral representations alone do not satisfy the requirement — they must be confirmed in writing through the management representation letter. Choice A is incorrect because oral assurances, while useful during fieldwork discussions, do not meet AU-C 580''s requirement for written confirmation of representations about matters material to the financial statements.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: easy / L2 — Representation about access to all information and personnel
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Patterson & Reed CPA is completing the audit of Crestview Logistics for the year ended December 31, 2025. The engagement partner prepares the management representation letter for the client''s signature. Under AU-C 580, which representation about access to information should be included?', '["Management has provided the auditor with access to all information relevant to the preparation of the financial statements", "Management has allowed the auditor to access only the financial records specified in the engagement letter", "Management has provided access to information approved by the audit committee", "Management has granted full access to all company records including attorney-client privileged communications"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 580.11, a required representation states that management has provided the auditor with all information relevant to the financial statements and any additional information requested, along with unrestricted access to persons within the entity. Choice B is incorrect because the representation is broader than just records in the engagement letter — it covers all relevant information, not a limited subset predetermined at engagement acceptance.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Management responsibility for financial statements
(1, 'Written Representations', 'What does management acknowledge in the written representation letter regarding the financial statements?', '["That the financial statements have been reviewed by the audit committee", "That all accounting policies comply with industry best practices", "That the auditor bears primary responsibility for financial statement accuracy", "That management is responsible for the preparation and fair presentation of the financial statements"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 580.11, a required written representation confirms that management acknowledges its responsibility for the preparation and fair presentation of the financial statements in accordance with the applicable financial reporting framework. This is fundamental to the allocation of responsibilities between management and the auditor. Choice C is incorrect because the auditor is responsible for the audit opinion, not for the financial statements themselves — management retains primary responsibility for fair presentation.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Entity-specific tailored representations
(1, 'Written Representations', 'During the audit of Pinnacle Biotech for the year ended December 31, 2025, the engagement team at Ashford & Lang determines that the company holds $4.8 million in Level 3 fair value instruments and has significant capitalized research and development costs. The engagement partner decides to request additional representations beyond the standard requirements. Under AU-C 580, entity-specific representations are:', '["Prohibited because the standard specifies an exhaustive list of required representations", "Limited to matters involving litigation and regulatory compliance only", "Appropriate when the auditor determines they are necessary to support other audit evidence", "Required only for entities with total assets exceeding $10 million"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 580.13, in addition to the required representations, the auditor may request additional written representations about matters specific to the entity''s industry or circumstances. Entity-specific representations are appropriate whenever the auditor determines they are necessary to support audit evidence obtained. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 580 does not limit representations to a fixed list — the auditor exercises judgment to request additional representations tailored to the engagement.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Representations about significant assumptions in estimates
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Evergreen Manufacturing reports a $3.5 million warranty reserve based on management''s estimate of future claims for the year ended December 31, 2025. The engagement team at Coleman & Hayes requests a representation about the reasonableness of the significant assumptions used in this estimate. Under AU-C 580, what should management''s representation about accounting estimates include?', '["A certification that the estimate will not require adjustment in future periods", "An assertion that the estimate was computed by an independent third-party specialist", "A statement that all estimates are based on industry averages and require no individual judgment", "A statement that the methods, data, and significant assumptions used in making accounting estimates are reasonable in the context of the applicable financial reporting framework"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 580.11, management should represent that the methods, significant assumptions, and data used in making accounting estimates are reasonable within the applicable financial reporting framework. This covers the reasonableness of inputs and judgment, not guarantees about future outcomes. Choice A is incorrect because no representation can certify an estimate''s future accuracy — estimates inherently involve uncertainty, and the representation addresses the reasonableness of the inputs, not the precision of the result.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Discovery contradicting compliance representation
(1, 'Written Representations', 'During the audit of Keystone Pharmaceuticals for the year ended December 31, 2025, the engagement team at Merritt & Collins obtains management''s written representation that the company has complied with all aspects of laws and regulations having a material effect on the financial statements. Two months later, before the report is issued, a staff auditor discovers that Keystone received an FDA warning letter in October 2025 that was not disclosed. How should this discovery affect the auditor''s evaluation of the written representations?', '["The auditor should consider whether other representations may also be unreliable and perform additional procedures", "The discovery has no effect because the representation was accurate as of the date it was signed", "The auditor should accept management''s explanation that the warning letter was immaterial", "The auditor should reissue the representation letter with updated language"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 580.A27, when information contradicts management''s written representations, the auditor should reconsider the reliability of all representations and perform additional audit procedures as necessary. The undisclosed FDA warning letter directly contradicts the compliance representation and raises concerns about management''s integrity. Choice B is incorrect because the representation covers conditions existing at year-end — the October 2025 warning letter existed before the representation was signed, making the representation inaccurate.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Uncorrected misstatements representation
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Under AU-C 580, what must management represent regarding uncorrected misstatements identified during the audit?', '["That management believes the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in the aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole", "That no misstatements were identified during the audit", "That all misstatements were corrected before the financial statements were finalized", "That the auditor is responsible for determining whether uncorrected misstatements are material"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 580.11, management must represent that the effects of any uncorrected misstatements aggregated by the auditor during the audit are immaterial, both individually and in the aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole. A summary of these items is typically attached to the letter. Choice B is incorrect because the representation acknowledges uncorrected misstatements may exist — it addresses their immateriality rather than denying their existence.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Evaluating going concern representations against contradictory evidence
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Cascade Retail Group''s December 31, 2025 financial statements are prepared on a going concern basis. Management''s representation letter states that plans to address recurring losses and negative cash flows are feasible and will allow continued operations. The engagement team at Weston & Park reviews management''s cash flow projections, which assume a $6 million credit facility renewal that has not been approved by the bank. The bank has informally expressed concerns about Cascade''s creditworthiness. The engagement partner must evaluate the appropriateness of relying on this representation. What is the best course of action?', '["Accept the representation because management has the best insight into its own plans and intentions", "Issue an unmodified opinion because the representation is sufficient evidence of going concern viability", "Evaluate whether the going concern representation, combined with the uncertainty about the credit facility, warrants additional procedures and a possible modification to the opinion", "Withdraw from the engagement because the going concern uncertainty makes the representation unreliable"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 580 and AU-C 570, the auditor must evaluate management''s going concern representations in light of all available evidence. When the plans underlying a going concern assertion depend on uncertain future events such as an unapproved credit facility, the auditor must perform additional procedures, assess the adequacy of disclosures, and consider the effect on the opinion. Choice A is incorrect because the auditor cannot simply accept management''s assertion when contradictory evidence exists — professional skepticism requires independent evaluation of the feasibility of management''s plans.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — Prior-period representations in initial audit
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Thornberry & Associates accepts the initial audit of Lakeview Hospitality for the year ended December 31, 2025. Lakeview presents comparative financial statements with the prior year ended December 31, 2024, which was audited by a predecessor firm. The engagement partner must determine what periods the management representation letter should cover. Under AU-C 580, what representations should the auditor obtain for the prior period?', '["Representations covering the prior period if the current auditor is expressing an opinion on those financial statements", "No representations are needed for the prior period because it was audited by the predecessor", "Representations covering only events occurring after the predecessor''s report date", "Representations limited to confirming that the predecessor''s audit findings were addressed"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 580.15, the written representation letter should address all periods referred to in the auditor''s report. If the current auditor is expressing an opinion on prior-period financial statements, representations covering those periods are required regardless of whether a predecessor audited them. Choice B is incorrect because the predecessor''s involvement does not relieve management of the obligation to provide representations to the current auditor for any period on which an opinion is being expressed.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: hard / L4 — Attorney letter vs management representation letter for litigation
(1, 'Written Representations', 'During the audit of Sterling Defense Systems for the year ended December 31, 2025, the engagement team at Whitmore & Crane obtains management''s representation that all known litigation, claims, and assessments have been disclosed. The auditor also received an attorney letter from Sterling''s external counsel that identifies a $5.8 million patent infringement claim assessed as "reasonably possible" but does not mention a separate $900,000 employment dispute that management disclosed verbally. The engagement partner must evaluate whether the evidence is sufficient. What is the best course of action?', '["Rely on the management representation letter as sufficient evidence for all litigation disclosures", "Accept the attorney letter as complete because attorneys have a professional obligation to disclose all material claims", "Investigate the discrepancy by inquiring with the attorney about the omitted employment dispute and requesting an updated or supplementary attorney response", "Exclude the employment dispute from consideration because it was not confirmed by the attorney"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 580 and AU-C 501, the auditor uses both management representations and attorney letters to evaluate litigation disclosures, but neither source alone is sufficient. When a discrepancy exists between the two sources, the auditor must follow up with the attorney to determine why the employment dispute was omitted and obtain sufficient appropriate evidence. Choice B is incorrect because an attorney letter may not be exhaustive — the scope depends on the inquiry letter, and omissions do not necessarily mean claims do not exist.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Representation that all transactions have been recorded
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Under AU-C 580, what does management''s representation about recording of transactions confirm?', '["That all transactions are recorded at fair value in the financial statements", "That all transactions subject to the applicable financial reporting framework have been recorded in the financial statements", "That transactions are recorded only when cash has been received or paid", "That all transactions have been verified by an independent third party before recording"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 580.11, management represents that all transactions have been recorded and are reflected in the financial statements in accordance with the applicable financial reporting framework. This supports the completeness assertion by confirming no transactions were intentionally or inadvertently omitted. Choice C is incorrect because the representation refers to accrual-basis recording under the applicable framework, not cash-basis recognition — transactions are recorded when they meet framework recognition criteria.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Written representations cannot substitute for other procedures
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Under AU-C 580, which statement best describes written representations from management?', '["Provide necessary audit evidence but do not substitute for other audit procedures the auditor considers necessary", "May replace substantive testing when management is considered reliable", "Are optional if the auditor obtains sufficient evidence through other procedures alone", "Serve as the primary source of audit evidence for all financial statement assertions"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 580.04, written representations provide necessary audit evidence but do not replace other audit procedures that the auditor determines are necessary to obtain sufficient appropriate evidence. Representations complement, but never substitute for, substantive testing and other procedures. Choice B is incorrect because regardless of management''s reliability, representations cannot replace substantive testing — the auditor must obtain corroborating evidence through independent procedures.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Representation about subsequent events and omitted disclosure
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Brookfield Industries provides its management representation letter to the engagement team at Greystone & Associates, dated March 15, 2026, for the December 31, 2025 audit. The letter includes a representation that all events occurring subsequent to the balance sheet date requiring adjustment or disclosure have been addressed. On March 12, 2026, Brookfield''s largest customer filed for bankruptcy, which could impair $2.1 million in receivables. The representation letter does not mention this event. What is the most likely implication for the auditor?', '["The representation is valid because the customer bankruptcy falls outside the audit period", "The omission has no effect because subsequent events are addressed solely through AU-C 560 procedures", "The bankruptcy is a Type II event that requires no adjustment and can be ignored", "The omission indicates the representation may be incomplete, requiring the auditor to discuss the event with management and evaluate whether the letter should be revised"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 580.11 and AU-C 560, management must represent that subsequent events requiring adjustment or disclosure have been properly addressed. A customer bankruptcy on March 12 — before the representation letter date of March 15 — should have been considered. The omission indicates the representation is incomplete and must be addressed before the report is issued. Choice A is incorrect because the representation covers events through the letter date, and the bankruptcy occurred within that window.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Who provides written representations
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Under AU-C 580, from whom should the auditor request written representations?', '["Members of management with appropriate responsibilities for and knowledge of the matters addressed", "Any employee who has access to the accounting system", "Only external legal counsel for the entity", "The entity''s independent board members exclusively"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 580.09, the auditor requests written representations from members of management with appropriate responsibilities for the financial statements and knowledge of the matters concerned — typically the CEO and CFO or their equivalents. The key criterion is responsibility and knowledge, not title or tenure. Choice B is incorrect because representations come from senior management responsible for the financial statements, not from any employee with system access.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — Representation about no undisclosed side agreements
(1, 'Written Representations', 'During the audit of Magnolia Development Corp. for the year ended December 31, 2025, the engagement team at Hartwell & Stevens identifies a $7.2 million real estate sale completed in November 2025. The engagement partner requests that the management representation letter include a statement about side agreements. Under AU-C 580, what should management represent regarding this transaction?', '["That the transaction was reviewed and approved by the board of directors", "That there are no side agreements, oral or written, that modify the terms of the recorded transaction", "That the transaction was completed at fair market value as appraised by a third party", "That the purchaser''s creditworthiness has been independently verified"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 580.13, the auditor may request specific representations about whether side agreements exist that could modify the recorded terms of significant transactions. Undisclosed side agreements — such as buyback provisions, contingent pricing, or oral modifications — could materially affect the financial statements. Choice A is incorrect because board approval, while relevant to governance, is not the concern addressed by a side agreement representation — the focus is on whether hidden terms alter the substance of the transaction.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Fair value measurement representations and single pricing model
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Beacon Investments holds a portfolio of $12.4 million in Level 3 financial instruments measured at fair value for its December 31, 2025 financial statements. The engagement team at Langford & Shaw obtains management''s written representation that the fair value measurements and disclosures comply with ASC 820. The auditor discovers that management used a single pricing model without considering alternative valuation methodologies. How does this discovery affect the auditor''s evaluation of the representation?', '["The representation is sufficient because management selected the valuation method", "The discovery has no impact because Level 3 instruments have inherent measurement uncertainty", "The auditor should analyze whether the failure to consider alternative methodologies indicates the representation about fair value compliance may not be supportable", "The auditor should reject the representation and require management to engage a valuation specialist"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 580 and AU-C 540, the auditor evaluates whether management''s representations about fair value measurements are consistent with the evidence obtained. When management uses a single pricing model without considering alternatives for complex Level 3 instruments, the auditor must analyze whether the representation about ASC 820 compliance is adequately supported. Choice A is incorrect because management''s selection of a method does not automatically validate the representation — the auditor must assess whether the chosen approach produces reasonable measurements.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: hard / L4 — Group audit component management representations
(1, 'Written Representations', 'The engagement team at Whitfield & Associates is auditing Meridian Global Holdings, a multinational group, for the year ended December 31, 2025. Meridian has a significant component in Brazil that is audited by a component auditor. The component management provides a representation letter to the component auditor but refuses to provide representations directly to the group engagement team. The group engagement partner must evaluate whether the component''s representation to the component auditor is sufficient. What is the best course of action?', '["Accept the component management''s representation to the component auditor as sufficient for the group audit", "Require the component auditor to reissue the representation letter on the group engagement team''s letterhead", "Issue a disclaimer of opinion on the consolidated financial statements due to the scope limitation", "Evaluate whether the representations obtained by the component auditor address the group team''s needs and consider obtaining additional representations or performing additional procedures"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 580 and AU-C 600, the group engagement team must obtain sufficient appropriate evidence, including representations, for the group audit. When component management refuses direct representations, the group engagement partner should evaluate whether the component auditor''s representations adequately address group-level needs and determine what additional procedures are necessary. Choice C is incorrect because a disclaimer is premature — the group engagement partner should first evaluate available alternatives, such as reviewing the component auditor''s representation letter or obtaining supplementary information.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — Integrity concerns from deliberate omission in representation letter
(1, 'Written Representations', 'The engagement team at Crawford & Associates is completing the audit of Summit Ridge Manufacturing for the year ended December 31, 2025. During the process of obtaining the management representation letter, the auditor discovers that the CFO deliberately omitted a known $1.8 million related-party transaction from the initial draft. When confronted, the CFO claims it was an oversight. The CEO was aware of the transaction and did not intervene. The engagement partner must evaluate the appropriate response. What is the best course of action?', '["Accept the revised representation letter after the CFO includes the omitted transaction", "Document the omission as an isolated error and proceed with issuing the opinion", "Issue a qualified opinion citing the initially omitted representation as a disagreement with management", "Evaluate the effect of the deliberate omission on the reliability of all management representations and consider whether doubt about management''s integrity requires withdrawal from the engagement"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 580.20-.21, if the auditor concludes there is sufficient doubt about management''s integrity such that the written representations are not reliable, the auditor should consider the effect on the audit opinion, which may include disclaiming an opinion or withdrawing from the engagement. A deliberate omission of a known related-party transaction raises serious integrity concerns. Choice A is incorrect because simply correcting the letter does not address the underlying integrity concern — the deliberate nature of the omission suggests other representations may also be unreliable.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Documentation of representations in audit workpapers
(1, 'Written Representations', 'The engagement team at Blair & Hudson completes the audit of Oakmont Financial Services for the year ended December 31, 2025. The audit manager asks how the management representation letter should be documented in the audit workpapers. Under AU-C 580, the representation letter is:', '["Maintained separately from the audit workpapers in the firm''s correspondence files", "Included in the audit documentation as evidence supporting the auditor''s conclusions", "Filed only if the auditor issues a modified opinion on the financial statements", "Retained in the permanent file and excluded from the current-year workpapers"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 580 and AU-C 230, the written representation letter is included in the audit documentation for the engagement. It serves as audit evidence and must be retained in the current-year workpapers along with other supporting documentation. Choice A is incorrect because the representation letter is an integral part of the audit documentation, not a separate correspondence file — it evidences a required audit procedure and supports the auditor''s conclusions.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Representation about providing minutes and records
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Under AU-C 580, which representation addresses management''s obligation regarding corporate governance records?', '["That board meetings are held at least quarterly throughout the fiscal year", "That the audit committee has reviewed all proposed adjusting entries", "That all minutes of meetings of shareholders, directors, and committees have been made available to the auditor", "That corporate governance records are maintained in compliance with SEC regulations"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 580.11, management represents that all minutes of meetings of shareholders, the board of directors, and committees of the board have been made available to the auditor. This supports the auditor''s ability to identify matters affecting the financial statements discussed at governance meetings. Choice A is incorrect because the representation addresses providing access to existing minutes, not the frequency of board meetings — meeting frequency is a governance matter, not an audit representation.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Representation about internal control responsibility
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Under AU-C 580, what does management represent regarding internal control?', '["That management acknowledges its responsibility for designing, implementing, and maintaining internal control relevant to the preparation and fair presentation of financial statements", "That internal controls have been tested by an independent third party", "That internal controls eliminate all risk of material misstatement", "That internal controls have been evaluated by the audit committee annually"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 580.11, management represents its acknowledgment of responsibility for the design, implementation, and maintenance of internal control relevant to the preparation and fair presentation of financial statements free from material misstatement. This is a required representation in every audit. Choice C is incorrect because internal controls provide reasonable, not absolute, assurance — no system of controls can eliminate all risk of material misstatement.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: hard / L3 — Contingency representations and reliance on in-house counsel
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Delta Aerospace Systems provides its management representation letter for the December 31, 2025 audit to the engagement team at Foster & Wynne. The letter includes a representation that all known contingencies and commitments have been disclosed. Delta operates in a heavily regulated industry and has ongoing government contract disputes. The auditor notes that management''s representation about contingencies relies heavily on in-house counsel''s assessment without input from external legal advisors. How should the auditor evaluate the sufficiency of evidence for contingencies?', '["The auditor should analyze whether reliance solely on in-house counsel provides sufficient appropriate evidence and may need to request that management authorize external counsel to communicate with the auditor", "The in-house counsel''s assessment is sufficient because counsel is part of management and knowledgeable about the entity''s legal matters", "The representation combined with in-house counsel''s assessment satisfies all evidence requirements for contingencies", "The auditor should refuse to accept the representation until all legal matters are fully resolved"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 580 and AU-C 501, while management''s representation about contingencies is necessary, it is not sufficient alone, particularly in complex legal environments. In-house counsel may lack objectivity, and the auditor should consider whether external legal confirmation is needed to corroborate management''s assertions about significant disputes. Choice B is incorrect because in-house counsel''s objectivity may be compromised by the employment relationship — in regulated industries with complex disputes, independent external confirmation provides more reliable evidence.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — Representation about accounting policy selection and changes
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Highland Manufacturing changes its depreciation method from double-declining balance to straight-line for all fixed assets effective January 1, 2025. The engagement team at Vincent & Graham prepares the management representation letter for Highland''s December 31, 2025 audit. During evaluation, the senior auditor questions how the accounting policy change interacts with the required representation about policy selection. What is the most likely effect of the change on the representation?', '["The change voids the representation about accounting policies until the SEC approves the new method", "The representation should confirm that the change in accounting policy is appropriate and in accordance with the applicable financial reporting framework", "The representation is unaffected because depreciation method changes are routine and require no special disclosure", "The auditor must refuse to accept the representation until the policy change has been in effect for two fiscal years"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 580.11, management represents that it has selected and applied accounting policies appropriately and consistently with the applicable financial reporting framework. When a policy change occurs, the representation should address the appropriateness of the new method and its conformity with the framework, including proper disclosure of the change. Choice C is incorrect because a depreciation method change is a change in accounting estimate effected by a change in method, which requires disclosure — the representation must address this change specifically.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Representation about related-party transaction completeness
(1, 'Written Representations', 'During the audit of Copperfield Holdings for the year ended December 31, 2025, the engagement team at Rayburn & Associates discovers that the CEO''s brother-in-law owns a vendor that provided $420,000 in consulting services during the year. The auditor requests a specific representation about related-party transactions. Under AU-C 580, management should represent that:', '["All related-party relationships and transactions have been identified and disclosed to the auditor in accordance with the requirements of the applicable financial reporting framework", "Related-party transactions are limited to those exceeding $500,000 in total annual value", "Only related-party transactions with the CEO and CFO require disclosure in the representation letter", "Related-party transactions have been conducted at fair market value as verified by an independent appraiser"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 580.11 and AU-C 550, management must represent that all related-party relationships and transactions have been appropriately identified, accounted for, and disclosed in accordance with the applicable financial reporting framework. This representation has no dollar threshold — all related-party transactions must be identified. Choice B is incorrect because there is no minimum dollar amount that exempts related-party transactions from the representation — completeness requires disclosure of all such relationships regardless of size.', 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
